WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
DIRE STATE:
This man has 1.2 billion subscribers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:10.920
What channel does he run? Well, he runs our&nbsp;
favorite channels 5 Minute Crafts, 123 Go!,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:18.300
Bright Side, Slick Slime Sam, La La Life, and Baby&nbsp;
Zoo just to name a few. All of these channels are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:24.300
owned by a corporation called The Soul Publishing&nbsp;
which is run by our protagonist Pavel Radaev.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.760
The company was only founded 7 years ago, but&nbsp;
given that they post hundreds of videos per day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:36.180
across 21 languages, they’ve essentially been&nbsp;
able to brute force their way to the top. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.580
while things look good on the surface, underneath&nbsp;
the hood, things aren’t going that great. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:47.580
they’re looking pretty bad. Take 5 minute crafts&nbsp;
for example. The channel has a whopping 80 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:53.580
subscribers but a lot of their videos can’t even&nbsp;
break 100k view. It’s even worse at Bright Side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.580 --> 00:01:00.780
A total of 45 million subscribers but many videos&nbsp;
can’t even break 20k views. Zooming out a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:06.180
Bright Side used to pull in 150 to 200 million&nbsp;
views per month back in their hayday but now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.700
the channel is down to 40 to 50 million views&nbsp;
per month. Not a bad amount but given that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.820
post 3 times a day, it’s very possible that this&nbsp;
operation is now unprofitable. A similar story can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.980
be seen with their subscriber count as well. Since&nbsp;
last October, Bright Side has had multiple months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:28.380
where their net subscriber gain was straight up&nbsp;
0. Clearly things aren’t looking great from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:34.200
business perspective but there’s more to it than&nbsp;
just that. You see, it’s easy to dismiss the Soul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.340
Publishing as just some soulless content farm&nbsp;
that’s looking to milk social media for views.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:45.300
But, when you take a deeper look, a much darker&nbsp;
agenda becomes more evident. Their quirky content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:51.000
is actually laced with politics and pro Russian&nbsp;
versions of history. One of their now deleted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.160
videos for example, claimed that Ukraine is part&nbsp;
of Russia back in 2019. Another claimed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.620
Alaska was a gift to the US from the Soviet Union.&nbsp;
And when you consider that the company is run by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:07.140
a bunch of Russian nationals, this all starts to&nbsp;
make sense. This means that the Soul Publishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.480
may not just be the world’s largest content&nbsp;
farm but also the world’s largest propaganda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:19.680
machine. So, here’s the controversial rise and&nbsp;
fall of the biggest YouTube empire of all time.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.600
SHADY BEGINNINGS?:&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:31.860
Taking a look back, the story of the Soul&nbsp;
Publishing indeed only dates back to 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.100
but the story of the company behind the company,&nbsp;
AdMe, dates back to the early 2000s to two men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:44.880
named Pavel Radaev and Marat Mukhametov. AdMe&nbsp;
started off as a media platform that seeked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.520
offer serious informational content. But, over&nbsp;
time this evolved into being more light hearted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:56.280
content focused on entertainment as this simply&nbsp;
appealed to a far larger audience. They basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.900
became the Russian BuzzFeed and would eventually&nbsp;
transition this style over to their dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.300
YouTube channels which brings us into our first&nbsp;
controversy: the timeline of the Soul Publishing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.620
After being a Russian focused media&nbsp;
organization for over a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.560
AdMe decided to launch the Soul Publishing in&nbsp;
2016. This has prompted some to theorize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.600
the Soul Publishing was created to influence&nbsp;
the 2016 election. At one point even Forbes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.960
and Business Insider explored the idea but these&nbsp;
claims don’t hold up against further scrutiny.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:33.840
For starters, many of the Soul Publishing’s most&nbsp;
popular channels were actually created after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.060
election. 5 Minute Crafts for example was created&nbsp;
a week after the election and Bright Side was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.220
created months after the election. Also, even for&nbsp;
channels that were created before the election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.500
they simply didn’t have much time to gain momentum&nbsp;
and influence the election even if that was their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.600
goal. The reality was that the Soul Publishing&nbsp;
was just putting out a bunch of corny videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:02.460
like these. 35 unbelievable cooking hacks. 29&nbsp;
things we do wrong every day. And 5 Minute Crafts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:09.240
Compilation. To be honest, these videos carried&nbsp;
no real value whatsoever as most of the time the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:15.540
hacks didn’t even make sense. I mean like what&nbsp;
even is this? The lack of functionality didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.920
really hurt the channels though. They had some&nbsp;
great titles and thumbnails that got people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:26.760
click and back in 2017 and 2018, that was really&nbsp;
all you needed. Also, they were posting dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:34.020
videos every single day so even if 99% of videos&nbsp;
went nowhere, as long as 1% went super viral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:39.540
that was all they needed. It didn’t take long for&nbsp;
reaction channels to start clowning on Bright Side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:45.780
and 5 Minute Crafts but this simply gave them even&nbsp;
more publicity and exposure. Before you knew it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:50.700
each of their channels were racking up tens of&nbsp;
millions of subscribers. Bright Side was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.860
reach over 30 million subscribers within a matter&nbsp;
of 3 years and 5 Minute Crafts was able to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:01.080
over 60 million subscribers within the same&nbsp;
time period. Also, it wasn’t just YouTube that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.480
they were blowing up on. They also had a massive&nbsp;
presence on Facebook, TikTok, Instagram, Snapchat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:13.260
and even Pinterest. By the end of 2019, they were&nbsp;
publishing 1500 videos per month with the help of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:20.880
550 employees. This was literally the epitome&nbsp;
of a content farm but I mean who could blame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:27.900
them. They were making millions posting useless&nbsp;
but also harmless content. Unfortunately though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:33.240
this hayday didn’t last long as the company’s&nbsp;
first tribulations were just around the corner.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:34.920
THE ALGORITHM SHIFTS:&nbsp;

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:45.960
The company’s first major challenge came from none&nbsp;
other than YouTube’s infamous algorithm. As we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:51.900
moved into the 2020s, YouTube started an internal&nbsp;
campaign against clickbait. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:57.720
YouTube was not exactly a fan of clickbait as it&nbsp;
simply gave the entire platform a cheap tabloid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.240
vibe. Not to mention, it likely led to people&nbsp;
spending less time on the platform. This wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:08.640
originally a major concern as YouTube’s user base&nbsp;
was simply growing so much. But, as the platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.320
started to mature, maximizing revenue from current&nbsp;
users became a larger priority than trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:20.400
attract new users. So, YouTube started focusing&nbsp;
on a new metric: average view duration. The longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.440
people watched your videos, the more likely it&nbsp;
was to be seen by more people. So naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.540
videos that were based around stories and payoffs&nbsp;
started taking off, and we say creators lean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:35.760
towards longer and longer videos. This, however,&nbsp;
was terrible news for the Soul Publishing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.580
Virtually all of their content was centered around&nbsp;
a gimmicky hook similar to what we see on TikTok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:46.800
today. So, once viewers clicked on the video and&nbsp;
saw the hack they came for, they clicked off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.440
They didn’t care about the other 20 hacks in&nbsp;
the video. This meant that while channels like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:58.020
Bright Side had a great click through rate, they&nbsp;
had awful viewer retention. As such, the Soul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:03.600
would decide to change up their content with a new&nbsp;
channel called Actually Happened. Most of you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:09.180
probably completely forgotten about this channel&nbsp;
as it has since been deleted so let me remind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:17.940
about the cringe content they posted. My BF has 74&nbsp;
GFs. I Date Only Psychos. BFF Broke Her Own Leg so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:22.740
She Can Be The Center Of Attention! Now, these&nbsp;
videos are actually from another channel called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:29.520
MyStoryAnimated but Actually happened posted the&nbsp;
some sort of stuff. While this is no doubt cringe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.220
it’s once again effective at getting people to&nbsp;
click. People like hearing about drama and gossip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:41.460
Combine this with a storyline and characters and&nbsp;
boom people are more suddenly invested in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:46.200
videos than their own financial futures. This&nbsp;
deadly combination of super high click through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:51.960
rates and ridiculous watch time allowed Actually&nbsp;
Happened to basically blow up overnight but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:57.540
wasn’t all sunshines and rainbows. You see, the&nbsp;
channel was so cringe that it was successful at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:03.960
creating a hate club also over night. People loved&nbsp;
to clown on just how fake their stories were and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:09.780
honestly you can blame the critics. I think I&nbsp;
remember a video where the kid didn’t want the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:14.220
dad to drop them off at school because the dad was&nbsp;
gonna drop them off in a helicopter or some crap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:20.220
like. Yeah, it was really weird. There were also&nbsp;
complaints that the channel was a bad influence as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:26.400
it sometimes glorified abusive relationships and&nbsp;
cheaters. I mean just listen to this. "I Was Going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:34.740
To Marry My Gf Until I Met My First Love. I'm A&nbsp;
Cheater". But out of nowhere, the Soul decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:39.360
to wipe this channel completely off the face&nbsp;
of the planet as they deleted literally every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:45.780
single video on the channel. There was never an&nbsp;
official explanation for this but it was probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:51.060
just because it got the company too much bad&nbsp;
press. Viewers quickly moved on and forgot about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:57.360
Acutally Happened but the Soul was back to ground&nbsp;
zero and YouTube was about to evolve even more.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.000
LEFT BEHIND:
As YouTube started focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:10.620
on average view duration, creators naturally&nbsp;
started trying to play this system as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.120
They would artificially increase the length of&nbsp;
videos to skew the watch time. They would hide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.020
gift cards throughout the video so that people&nbsp;
would to watch the video over and over again even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:24.660
if it sucked. Again, this is not what YouTube had&nbsp;
in mind when they started focusing on watch time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:29.580
If YouTube really wanted to maximize the time&nbsp;
that people spent on YouTube, they had to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:35.640
on promoting videos that people actually liked&nbsp;
not just videos that people watched longer. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:40.380
YouTube implemented yet another key metric&nbsp;
into the algorithm which is of course viewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:46.140
satisfaction. The most blatant implementation of&nbsp;
this is the smiley face surveys but that’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:51.660
1 of likely thousands of variables. For example,&nbsp;
they probably measure how long we watch a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:56.700
before getting distracted and scrolling down to&nbsp;
the comments. They probably also measure how our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:03.000
viewing behavior is different between videos and&nbsp;
who knows what else. The bottomline though is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.400
what YouTube is looking for are happy viewers&nbsp;
which the Soul doesn’t really have. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:13.500
there’s virtually no one that’s actually excited&nbsp;
to watch a new video from Bright Side. At best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.780
people are looking to burn time. So, I can’t&nbsp;
imagine that the Soul’s viewer satisfaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:24.360
metrics are all that great. This wasn’t too big&nbsp;
of an issue back in 2020 when YouTube still had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:30.120
an emphasis on watch time and the Soul was able to&nbsp;
engineer watch time. But, as YouTube has shifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.440
more and more towards viewer satisfaction, the&nbsp;
Soul started seeing their viewership drastically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:39.540
decline. This was also around the time that&nbsp;
short form content started to explode and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:45.120
everyone was jumping on the band wagon especially&nbsp;
becuase of the pandemic. YouTube launched shorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:51.480
Instagram launched Reels, and TikTok, well TikTok&nbsp;
was TikTok. Much of the demographic that watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:56.160
the Soul shifted over to short form content which&nbsp;
makes sense given that it feeds into the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:02.040
tendencies. Instant gratification, quick tricks&nbsp;
and tips, and a bunch of dopamine. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:07.080
with short form content, you usually got&nbsp;
a splash of personality as well as opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:12.000
to some random voice actor working for the&nbsp;
Soul Publishing machine. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.280
viewers started ditching the Soul’s channels&nbsp;
in the masses leading us to where we are today.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:19.320
NO SOUL:&nbsp;

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:30.600
Today, the Soul Publishing isn’t exactly doing&nbsp;
bad as they still have ridiculous followings. 600&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:36.900
million on Facebook, 1.2 billion on YouTube, 90&nbsp;
million on TtikTok, 70 million on Instagram, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:44.160
20 million on SnapChat. But with that being said,&nbsp;
the company’s hayday is definitely behind us and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:50.400
their insane follower counts are starting to mean&nbsp;
less and less. The reality is that their followers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:56.040
are simply no longer converting into viewers. And&nbsp;
thanks to the YouTube algorithm, this effect will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.040 --> 00:12:01.260
simply get worse with time. As their newer videos&nbsp;
get fewer views, YouTube will promote their future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:07.140
videos even less which will simply make it even&nbsp;
harder to get views. So, it definitely seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:12.300
it’s just a matter of time until this content farm&nbsp;
or propaganda machine or whatever it is becomes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:18.660
blip in internet history and that’s the story&nbsp;
of the 1.2 billion sub YouTuber that’s losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:23.640
it all. Someone else that’s at risk of losing&nbsp;
it all is short form platforms. If you’d like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.540
to see why companies are already giving up&nbsp;
on short form content, check out this video.

